## Solution

**Setup.** Let $R = \mathbb{Q}[x]$, and let $A = \begin{bmatrix} x-1 & 0 \\ 1-x & x^2 \end{bmatrix}$ define the map $R^2 \xrightarrow{A} R^2$. Then $M = \operatorname{coker}(A) = R^2 / \operatorname{im}(A)$. We compute $\operatorname{Hom}_R(M, R/(x^2))$.

**Step 1: Reduce Hom to a kernel.** The exact sequence $R^2 \xrightarrow{A} R^2 \to M \to 0$ yields, by contravariance of $\operatorname{Hom}_R(-, N)$ with $N = R/(x^2)$,
$$0 \to \operatorname{Hom}_R(M, N) \to \operatorname{Hom}_R(R^2, N) \xrightarrow{A^*} \operatorname{Hom}_R(R^2, N).$$
Identifying $\operatorname{Hom}_R(R^2, N) \cong N^2$ (column vectors, $f \leftrightarrow (f(e_1), f(e_2))$), the map $A^*$ is precomposition by $A$, which acts as $v \mapsto A^T v$. Hence
$$\operatorname{Hom}_R(M, N) \cong \ker\!\big(A^T : N^2 \to N^2\big).$$

**Step 2: Compute $A^T$ over $N = R/(x^2)$.** We have
$$A^T = \begin{bmatrix} x-1 & 1-x \\ 0 & x^2 \end{bmatrix}.$$
In $N = R/(x^2)$, $x^2 = 0$, so
$$A^T \equiv \begin{bmatrix} x-1 & 1-x \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} x-1 & -(x-1) \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}.$$
The map sends $(n_1, n_2) \mapsto \big((x-1)(n_1 - n_2),\; 0\big)$.

**Step 3: Determine the kernel.** The kernel consists of $(n_1, n_2) \in N^2$ with $(x-1)(n_1 - n_2) = 0$ in $R/(x^2)$.

**Key observation:** $x - 1$ is a **unit** in $R/(x^2)$. Indeed, its constant term is $-1 \neq 0$, and
$$(x-1)(-1-x) = -(x-1)(1+x) = -(x^2 - 1) = 1 - x^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{x^2}.$$
So $(x-1)^{-1} = -1 - x$ in $R/(x^2)$.

Since $x-1$ is a unit, $(x-1)(n_1 - n_2) = 0$ forces $n_1 - n_2 = 0$, i.e. $n_1 = n_2$.

Therefore
$$\ker(A^T) = \{(n, n) : n \in R/(x^2)\} \cong R/(x^2).$$

**Step 4: Compute the $\mathbb{Q}$-rank.** As a $\mathbb{Q}$-vector space,
$$R/(x^2) \cong \mathbb{Q}\{1,\, x\},$$
which has dimension $2$.

**Conclusion.**
$$\operatorname{Hom}_R(M, R/(x^2)) \cong R/(x^2) \cong \mathbb{Q}^2,$$
so the rank of the $\mathbb{Q}$-vector space $\operatorname{Hom}_R(M, R/(x^2))$ is $\boxed{2}$.

